-- title:
--   Ogg's modular unit lies in ℚ(j(q),j(q^N))
-- statement:
--   Let $N$ be a natural number with $N \neq 0$. Inside the field of formal Laurent series $\mathbb{Q}((q))$, written here as `LaurentSeries ℚ` (Hahn series over $\mathbb{Q}$ with integer exponents), consider the series $\Delta$-type element `deltaSeries`, namely the product of the monomial $q^{1}$ with the image of the power series `dedekindEtaUnitQ` under the inclusion of power series into Laurent series, and the ring endomorphism `qExpand ℚ N` of `LaurentSeries ℚ` obtained by pushing the support forward along multiplication by $N$ on the exponent group $\mathbb{Z}$, i.e. the substitution $q \mapsto q^{N}$. The element `modularUnitSeries N` is the quotient $\mathtt{deltaSeries} \cdot (\mathtt{qExpand}\ \mathbb{Q}\ N\ \mathtt{deltaSeries})^{-1}$. The assertion is that this element belongs to `modularFunctionField N`, the intermediate field of $\mathbb{Q}((q))$ generated over $\mathbb{Q}$ by the two elements `jq` and `qExpand ℚ N jq`, that is, by the $q$-series `jq` and its image under $q \mapsto q^{N}$. Thus $\Delta(q)/\Delta(q^{N})$ is a rational function, with rational coefficients, of $j(q)$ and $j(q^{N})$, for every level $N \geq 1$, prime or composite.
--
--   This is the formal $q$-expansion form of the classical fact that the modular unit $\Delta(\tau)/\Delta(N\tau)$ on $X_0(N)$ lies in the function field $\mathbb{Q}(j, j_N)$ of that curve, generated by only the two expansions $j(q)$ and $j(q^N)$ rather than by all $j(q^d)$ with $d \mid N$. It is used in the construction of separated coverings of chart contexts, [`ModularCurve.MultCovering.exists_chartCtx_separated_covering`](thm.html#ModularCurve.MultCovering.exists_chartCtx_separated_covering).
